{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And Jacob lived in the land of Egypt seventeen years.</b> I have already mentioned (Ramban on Genesis 43:14) that Jacob's descent to Egypt is our exile today at the hand of the fourth beast (Daniel 7:7), the dominion of Rome — for the sons of Jacob themselves brought about their descent there through the sale of Joseph their brother, and Jacob went down there because of the famine and thought to find salvation with his son in the house of one who loved him, for Pharaoh loved Joseph and regarded him as a son. They intended to go up from there when the famine ended from the land of Canaan, as they said (Genesis 47:4), 'Sojourn in the land we came, for there is no pasture for the flocks that belong to your servants, for the famine is heavy in the land of Canaan.' But they did not go up; instead, the exile grew long upon them, and he died there, and his bones were brought up, and the elders of Pharaoh and his officers brought him up and observed for him a heavy mourning. So too we, with Rome and Edom our brothers, they brought about our entry into their hand, for they made a covenant with the Romans, and Agrippa the last king of the Second Temple fled to them for help, and because of the famine the people of Jerusalem were captured. And the exile has grown very long upon us; its end is not known like the other exiles, and we are in it like the dead, saying, 'Our bones are dried up, our hope is lost' (Ezekiel 37:11). They will bring us up from all the nations as an offering to HaShem, and there will be for them a heavy mourning when they see our glory, and we shall see in the vengeance of HaShem; may He raise us up and we shall live before Him."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:46:07.613653036Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}